          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does antibiotic stewardship guidance say about upper respiratory infections?",
          "answer": "**What the guidance agrees on**\n\nThe main stewardship guidance, \"Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated,\" says that most upper respiratory infections are viral. These include the common cold, most sore throats, acute bronchitis, and uncomplicated sinusitis in its first days. Antibiotics do not shorten these illnesses. The guidance also says they cause harm, including side effects, allergic reactions, *C. difficile* infection, and antibiotic resistance. It treats not prescribing antibiotics as an active recommendation, not just the absence of one. It also says that explaining the expected course and offering symptom relief are part of the treatment. A general reference on the common cold makes the same point: supportive care is the usual approach.\n\n**Where authorities disagree**\n\nThe same guidance reports two disagreements:\n\n- **Delayed prescribing:** Some bodies recommend a delayed-prescription strategy as a tool to reduce immediate prescribing. Others are wary. They note that a prescription still results in a substantial fraction of cases, and that it may reinforce the idea that these illnesses need antibiotics.\n- **Sore throat:** Some national guidance gives more weight to clinical scoring and less to testing everyone before treatment. The retrieved text does not give the details of that scoring.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not name the specific bodies behind each position.\n\n**How strong the evidence is**\n\nThe retrieved passages do not grade the strength of evidence behind these recommendations. I can't tell you how confident the authorities are in each recommendation, so please check the original guidelines for that.\n\n**When to seek care**\n\nThe general reference says to get further assessment if symptoms get worse after improving, fever returns, symptoms last beyond ten days, or there is severe facial pain or one-sided facial swelling. It also lists severe sore throat with trouble swallowing or drooling, and any breathlessness. Seek urgent care right away for difficulty breathing, chest pain, a stiff neck with severe headache, confusion, or signs of dehydration.\n\nThis is general information, not a diagnosis. A clinician can advise on your own situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
